/// Chapter 26 of the guide, written from md-docs/recipes/offline.md by
/// scripts/site-from-guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page OfflineGuide(path: "/docs/guide/offline", title: "Offline", description: "Offline support: precaching routes, a fallback page, cache policies, writes kept for later, the update flow, testing it, and its limits.", layout: DocsShell("Offline", chapter: t("nav.group.data"), blurb: "Turn on a service worker the build writes for you: the site works with no network, writes wait for the connection, and new versions are offered, not forced.", source: "md-docs/recipes/offline.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "before-you-start", label: "Before you start"), Anchor(id: "turn-on-offline-support", label: "Turn on offline support"), Anchor(id: "keep-writes-made-offline", label: "Keep writes made offline"), Anchor(id: "offer-a-new-version", label: "Offer a new version"), Anchor(id: "what-the-worker-keeps", label: "What the worker keeps"), Anchor(id: "test-it-offline", label: "Test it offline"), Anchor(id: "troubleshooting", label: "Troubleshooting")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/realtime", prevLabel: t("ch.25"), nextTo: "/docs/guide/content", nextLabel: t("ch.27"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter shows you how to make your site work with no network. You add one `offline` section to the config, and the build writes a *service worker* — a script the browser runs beside your site, which answers the site's requests from a store on the reader's machine. Stored pages then load offline, writes made offline are sent when the connection returns, and a new deploy is offered to the reader rather than swapped in under them. You do not write the worker or name any cache by hand.")

        Heading("Before you start", id: "before-you-start").h2

        Markdown("- **Serve the site over HTTPS**, or from `localhost`. Browsers run service workers only from a secure origin.\n- **Serve `sw.js` fresh.** The page registers the worker with `updateViaCache: \"none\"`, so the browser asks for it on every visit — but a CDN in front can still hold a copy. Give `sw.js` the header `Cache-Control: no-cache` ([Deploying](/docs/guide/deploying#set-cache-headers)).\n- **Deploy everything in `build/` together.** The worker's version is a hash of every file the build wrote, so deploying half a build confuses it.")

        Heading("Turn on offline support", id: "turn-on-offline-support").h2

        Markdown("To store the site for when the network is gone, add `offline` to `webfluent.app.json`. The build then writes a service worker, `sw.js`, beside the pages:")

        CodeBlock(#"{ "offline": {
    "precache": ["/", "/docs/*"],
    "fallback": "/offline",
    "cache": { "/api/*": "network-first" },
    "sync": true
} }"#, title: "json")

        Table(caption: "Turn on offline support") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Key").header
                    Table.Cell("What it does").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`precache`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("The routes a first visit stores, as globs. `\"/\"` by default. Each route is stored with its own chunk and stylesheet, and the shell with them.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`fallback`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("A page's `path`, shown for a route that was not stored.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`cache`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("How a path the build did not write — an API, say — is fetched: `network-first`, `cache-first`, `stale-while-revalidate` or `network-only`. A path not named passes through untouched.") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`sync`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("Keep a write made with no network and send it later ([Keep writes made offline](#keep-writes-made-offline)).") }
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("A stored route loads with no network at all. When there is a network, a navigation still goes to it first, so a page that changed is never served stale while the server is there to ask.\n\nThe `fallback` is an ordinary page of your site. This one tells the reader what happened:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Offline(path: "/offline", title: "Offline", description: "Shown when a page was not stored.") {
    Heading("You are offline").h1
    Text("This page was not saved for offline use. It will load when you are back online.")
    Link("Go to the home page", to: "/")
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Keep writes made offline", id: "keep-writes-made-offline").h2

        Markdown("To keep the reader's changes when the network drops, set `\"sync\": true`. A write — any method but `GET` and `HEAD`, made through `fetch` or an `api` — that fails because the network is gone is then kept instead of thrown. The call resolves with nothing, so what the page showed stays shown.\n\nOnly a write that failed for want of a network is kept: the browser says it is offline, or the site's own server could not be reached. A request to another origin that is refused while the browser is online — a CORS refusal, a closed port — fails as it would without `sync`.\n\nA credential is never stored with a kept write. What an `api`'s `on request` hook adds — an `Authorization` header from the session — is added again when the write is sent, by the hook as it is then: a write made before a logout goes out with no one's token, never the old one. A write whose call carries a credential header itself (`headers: \{ \"Authorization\": token \}`) cannot be kept without it, so it is not kept, and its call fails as it would without `sync`.\n\nWhen the connection returns, the kept writes are sent in order:\n\n- by Background Sync, where the browser has it, so a closed tab still sends them;\n- by the page otherwise, the next time it is open.\n\nA server that answers has the write, even if it refuses it. A server that is down, or asks the client to wait, keeps the write waiting for later. A `File` body is not kept, and its call fails as it would without `sync`.\n\nA write whose service adds credentials with its hook is sent by a page that has that service open, so the worker leaves it — and the writes after it, to keep them in order — for the next such page.\n\nTo forget every kept write — when the reader logs out, say — call `WF.clearQueued()` from one of the project's scripts.\n\n`network.queued` is how many writes are waiting. Show it so the reader knows their changes are safe:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Notes(path: "/notes", title: "Notes", description: "Notes that save offline.") {
    Heading("Notes").h1
    if network.queued > 0 {
        Text("{network.queued} change(s) will be sent when you are back online").muted
    }
}"#, title: "wf")

        Heading("Offer a new version", id: "offer-a-new-version").h2

        Markdown("The worker is versioned by a hash of everything the build wrote, so any deploy that changes a byte is a new version. The new version installs beside the old one and waits. `update.available` turns true when it is ready, and `update.apply()` takes it. This home page offers a reload and shows the queue:")

        CodeBlock(#"page Home(path: "/", title: "Home", description: "The front page.") {
    if update.available {
        Button("A new version is ready — reload") { on click { update.apply() } }
    }
    if network.queued > 0 {
        Text("{network.queued} change(s) will be sent when you are back online").muted
    }
    Heading("Home").h1
}"#, title: "wf")

        Markdown("`update.apply()` takes the new version and reloads the page once. It never reloads on the first install, which only takes over a page that already works, and never twice for one update. The old version's store is deleted when the new one takes over.")

        Heading("What the worker keeps", id: "what-the-worker-keeps").h2

        Markdown("What a `cache` entry fetches is kept in the worker's runtime store, the 200 most recently fetched answers, for the next reader of the device — so an answer that is one reader's is never kept: one the server marks `Cache-Control: no-store` or `private`, one asked for with an `Authorization` header, and one that varies by `Cookie` or `Authorization`. Mark what is personal `private` and it stays off the device.\n\nTwo sites on one origin under different `base_path`s keep apart: each has its own stores, kept writes and `persist` values (`wf:/shop:cart`), and neither deletes, serves or sends the other's. A site at the root keeps the names it always had, and one under a base path reads a value kept under the old name once and keeps it under the new one.")

        Callout("Under `wf serve` the worker removes itself. A page answered from its own store would hide every edit you make.", kind: .note)

        Heading("Test it offline", id: "test-it-offline").h2

        Markdown("Because `wf serve` removes the worker on purpose, test the built output with a plain static server. Build the site and serve `build/`:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "wf build"),
                Line(command: "python3 -m http.server -d build")
            ])

        Markdown("Then, in the browser:\n\n1. Open the site. In the developer tools, open **Application → Service workers** and check the worker is activated.\n2. In the **Network** panel, tick **Offline** and reload. Stored routes load; every other route shows the `fallback` page.\n3. Make a write, then untick **Offline**. The write goes out, and `network.queued` drops to zero.\n\n`wf verify` runs against the built output too, and the repository's own browser tests hold the whole flow to a real Chrome.")

        Heading("Troubleshooting", id: "troubleshooting").h2

        Markdown("- **An upload made offline fails.** A `File` body is never kept: the call fails as it would without `sync`. Ask the reader to try again online.\n- **A write arrives at the server minutes late.** That is the design: a kept write is sent once it can be. Make the server's endpoints safe to receive late, and idempotent where a retry could repeat one.\n- **The API the site needs does not work offline.** What `cache` does not name passes straight through to the network. Give the API a `network-first` or `stale-while-revalidate` entry.\n- **A page that loaded offline yesterday has to be fetched again.** The browser decides how much it keeps, and can evict the store under storage pressure. The next visit with a network stores it again.\n- **Readers keep getting the old version.** Something in front of the site caches `sw.js`. Serve it with `Cache-Control: no-cache`.\n- **Nothing is stored when you test with `wf serve`.** The dev server removes the worker. Test the output of `wf build` with a static server.")
    }
}
